<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Makaʻāinana</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Makaainana, Maka'ainana</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The makaʻāinana were the commoners of ancient Hawaiʻi, who farmed, fished and practised the simpler crafts, labouring for their families and to support the chiefs (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alii">aliʻi</a>) and priests (kahuna). In Hawaiian belief all land belonged to the gods; the chiefs managed it and controlled the commoners who worked it. When a chief died or was defeated the lands were shared out anew, but commoners were usually left in their houses and fields, paying tribute and labour to the new chief under a new overseer (konohiki). Beneath them were only the kauwā: servants, slaves and outcasts.</p></div>
